C++ Base Pointer With Virtual Function
Description

This C++ program demonstrates run-time polymorphism using a base-class pointer and a virtual function.

The Animal class contains a virtual sound() function. The Dog and Cat classes inherit from Animal and provide their own implementations of the sound() function.

Classes Used
1. Animal Class

The Animal class is the base class.

It contains the virtual sound() function.
The function displays "Animal makes a sound".
It also contains a virtual destructor.
The virtual keyword enables run-time polymorphism.
2. Dog Class

The Dog class is derived from the Animal class.

It overrides the sound() function.
It displays "Dog barks".
The override keyword indicates that it overrides the base-class virtual function.
3. Cat Class

The Cat class is also derived from the Animal class.

It overrides the sound() function.
It displays "Cat meows".
Main Function

In the main() function, objects of Dog and Cat are created.

A base-class pointer is used:

Animal* animal;

First, the pointer points to the Dog object:

animal = &dog;

Then:

animal->sound();

calls the Dog version of sound().

After that, the same pointer points to the Cat object:

animal = &cat;

and:

animal->sound();

calls the Cat version of sound().

Output
Dog barks
Cat meows
OOP Concept Used

Run-Time Polymorphism — Virtual Function

              Animal
                |
          virtual sound()
             /      \
            /        \
         Dog          Cat
          |            |
      Dog barks     Cat meows

The same base-class pointer can point to different derived objects, and the appropriate derived-class function is selected during execution.